# Payment SDK Callback Doesn't Update UI (NgZone / Change Detection Diagnosis)

## 1. What is it?

This is a diagnosis-and-fix scenario about Angular's change detection not running after a third-party SDK's callback fires. The payment SDK is JavaScript that Angular doesn't control — it runs its success callback however it wants, often completely outside Angular's awareness. So even though your callback code correctly updates a component property, Angular never finds out that anything changed, so it never re-renders the DOM. The UI only catches up when something else (a click, which Angular *does* listen for) happens to trigger a change detection pass afterward.

## 2. Why was it introduced?

This isn't a feature — it's a consequence of how Angular's change detection is triggered in the first place, and it's worth understanding *why* that mechanism exists at all.

Angular doesn't magically know when your data changes. Historically (with Zone.js), Angular relies on monkey-patching every async browser API it knows about — `setTimeout`, `Promise`, DOM events, `XMLHttpRequest`, `fetch`, etc. Whenever one of these patched APIs completes, Zone.js notifies Angular, and Angular runs a change detection pass across the component tree to sync the DOM with the latest data.

This works great for code Angular controls. The problem: third-party SDKs (payment gateways, maps libraries, chat widgets, analytics scripts) are often loaded as external `<script>` tags before Zone.js patches things, or they use mechanisms Zone.js doesn't intercept — postMessage-based iframes, custom event emitters, native WebSocket callbacks in some setups, or code that explicitly escapes Angular's zone. When their callback fires and mutates your component state, Zone.js was never told about it, so Angular has no idea a change detection pass is needed. The data model is correct in memory — the DOM just hasn't been told to catch up.

## 3. How does it work?

**Diagnosis — why this happens:**
- The payment SDK likely runs its callback via a mechanism Zone.js doesn't patch (common with SDKs loaded from an external CDN, iframe `postMessage` handlers, or SDKs that explicitly call `Zone.root.run()` / use `NgZone.runOutsideAngular()` internally to avoid disrupting their own performance).
- Your callback updates a component field (e.g., `this.paymentStatus = 'success'`), which is correct — the JS value is genuinely updated.
- But because this happened *outside* Angular's zone, Angular's change detection was never triggered.
- The DOM stays stale until the next event Angular *does* intercept (a click anywhere) triggers a full change detection pass, which then also picks up the now-stale-but-correct data and finally renders it — making it look like "clicking anywhere fixes it," which is the classic symptom of this bug.

**The fix — force Angular back into the loop:**
- Inject `NgZone` and wrap the code that updates state with `this.ngZone.run(() => { ... })`. This manually re-enters Angular's zone, and when that callback finishes, Angular automatically triggers change detection — same as if the change had happened from an Angular-patched event to begin with.
- Alternative/complementary approach: inject `ChangeDetectorRef` and call `this.cdr.markForCheck()` (for `OnPush` components) or `this.cdr.detectChanges()` — this directly asks Angular to re-check this component's view, without needing to go through the zone at all.
- In modern **zoneless** Angular (signals-based change detection, no Zone.js), the fix looks different: if you're using a `signal()` for the state, writing to it (`this.paymentStatus.set('success')`) automatically notifies Angular's reactivity graph and schedules a render — regardless of what zone/context the write happened in. Signals sidestep this whole class of bug because they don't rely on Zone.js interception at all.

## 4. Example  -> working eg link --> https://stackblitz.com/edit/dt3ubztt-stoggwyl?file=src%2Fapp%2Fsignal-checkout.component.ts,src%2Fapp%2Fbuggy-checkout.component.ts,src%2Fapp%2Ffixed-checkout.component.ts,src%2Fapp%2Fapp.component.ts

```typescript
import { Component, NgZone, ChangeDetectorRef, OnInit } from '@angular/core';

declare const PaymentSDK: any; // third-party SDK, loaded via external <script>

@Component({
  selector: 'app-checkout',
  standalone: true,
  template: `
    <div *ngIf="paymentStatus === 'success'">✅ Payment confirmed!</div>
    <div *ngIf="paymentStatus === 'pending'">Processing payment...</div>
  `,
})
export class CheckoutComponent implements OnInit {
  paymentStatus: 'pending' | 'success' = 'pending';

  constructor(private ngZone: NgZone, private cdr: ChangeDetectorRef) {}

  ngOnInit(): void {
    // BUGGY version — SDK callback runs outside Angular's zone.
    // PaymentSDK.onSuccess(() => {
    //   this.paymentStatus = 'success'; // data updates, but view doesn't
    // });

    // FIXED version — manually re-enter Angular's zone
    PaymentSDK.onSuccess(() => {
      this.ngZone.run(() => {
        this.paymentStatus = 'success';
        // Angular now knows a change happened inside its zone,
        // so it automatically runs change detection after this callback exits.
      });
    });
  }
}

```

```typescript
// Alternative fix for OnPush components, or when you don't want
// to touch zone behavior at all — directly ask Angular to check this view.
PaymentSDK.onSuccess(() => {
  this.paymentStatus = 'success';
  this.cdr.markForCheck(); // schedules this component (and ancestors) for CD
  // or this.cdr.detectChanges() to run it synchronously right now
});
```

```typescript
// Zoneless / Signals version — no NgZone needed at all
import { signal } from '@angular/core';

export class CheckoutComponent {
  paymentStatus = signal<'pending' | 'success'>('pending');

  ngOnInit() {
    PaymentSDK.onSuccess(() => {
      // Writing to a signal notifies Angular's reactivity graph directly —
      // works correctly even though this callback runs outside any zone.
      this.paymentStatus.set('success');
    });
  }
}
```

## 5. Example Explanation / Execution Flow

**Buggy flow:**
1. `PaymentSDK.onSuccess(callback)` registers the callback with the SDK, which manages its own execution context (often outside Zone.js's patched APIs).
2. Payment completes. The SDK invokes the callback directly, outside Angular's zone.
3. `this.paymentStatus = 'success'` executes — the JavaScript object's property genuinely changes in memory.
4. Because this happened outside the zone, Zone.js never fires its "something async completed" notification, so Angular's change detection never runs.
5. The `*ngIf="paymentStatus === 'success'"` binding is still evaluating against the *old* rendered view — the DOM wasn't told to re-check.
6. User clicks anywhere on the page. That click *is* a Zone-patched DOM event, so Angular runs change detection as a side effect of handling it.
7. Change detection walks the component tree, re-evaluates bindings, sees `paymentStatus` is now `'success'`, and finally updates the DOM. This is why it "randomly" fixes itself on any click — it's not actually related to what was clicked.

**Fixed flow (with `ngZone.run`):**
1. SDK invokes the callback outside Angular's zone, same as before.
2. Instead of mutating state directly, the callback body runs inside `this.ngZone.run(() => {...})`.
3. `NgZone.run()` explicitly re-enters the Angular zone for the duration of that function.
4. Inside it, `this.paymentStatus = 'success'` executes.
5. When the `ngZone.run()` callback finishes, Angular (via Zone.js) automatically triggers a change detection pass, exactly as it would for any zone-patched event.
6. The DOM updates immediately — no extra click required.

## 6. When should we use it?

- Any integration with third-party SDKs: payment gateways (Stripe, Razorpay, PayPal), maps (Google Maps, Mapbox), chat widgets (Intercom, Zendesk), analytics callbacks, WebSocket libraries that manage their own connection lifecycle, or legacy jQuery plugins wrapped into Angular.
- Any time you see a bug described as "the UI updates only after I click/scroll/resize" — that's almost always a change-detection-didn't-run symptom, and the fix pattern is the same regardless of the specific trigger.
- Conversely, `NgZone.runOutsideAngular()` is the inverse tool — used deliberately to keep *high-frequency* operations (like a `mousemove` handler doing pixel-level drawing) from triggering excessive change detection cycles, for performance.

## 7. Advantages / Disadvantages

**Advantages**
- `ngZone.run()` is a minimal, surgical fix — one line wrapping the exact code that needs to notify Angular, no wider architectural changes needed.
- `ChangeDetectorRef.markForCheck()`/`detectChanges()` gives fine-grained control, especially useful with `OnPush` components where you don't want unrelated ancestor re-renders.
- Signals-based state avoids this entire class of bug by design, since they don't rely on Zone.js patching to know when to update.

**Disadvantages**
- Easy to forget — this bug is invisible until you specifically test the "does UI update immediately" case, since the data itself looks correct in the debugger.
- Overusing `ngZone.run()` everywhere "just in case" defeats its purpose and can mask the real question of which async sources are and aren't zone-aware.
- Zone.js itself adds overhead and complexity; this class of bug is one of the practical reasons Angular is moving toward zoneless change detection with signals.

## 8. Common Interview Traps

- Jumping straight to "add `cdr.detectChanges()` everywhere" without explaining *why* the bug happens — interviewers want to hear the zone/Zone.js reasoning, not just a memorized fix.
- Confusing `NgZone.run()` (re-enter the Angular zone, trigger CD) with `NgZone.runOutsideAngular()` (the opposite — deliberately skip CD for performance). Mixing these up in an answer is a red flag.
- Assuming this only happens with "old" Angular — it's still relevant with Zone.js-based apps (the default until zoneless matures), so don't wave it away as a solved/irrelevant problem.
- Not connecting the symptom ("works after any click") to the mechanism (any Zone-patched event triggers a *global* change detection pass, not just for the element clicked) — that connection is exactly what shows real understanding of Angular's change detection model.
- Forgetting that `OnPush` components need `markForCheck()` specifically — just running inside the zone isn't enough for `OnPush` if the state change didn't originate from an `@Input()` reference change, a template event, or an observable/async pipe emission.

## 9. Interview Follow-up Questions

**Q: What's the difference between `NgZone.run()` and `ChangeDetectorRef.detectChanges()`?**
Answer: `NgZone.run()` re-enters Angular's zone so that Zone.js's normal "something happened, run change detection globally" mechanism kicks in afterward. `detectChanges()` bypasses zones entirely and directly, synchronously re-checks a specific component's view (and its children) right now — useful when you don't want to rely on zone behavior at all, e.g., in `OnPush` components or zone-less setups.

**Q: Why doesn't Zone.js automatically patch every async API?**
Answer: Zone.js patches a known, finite set of browser APIs it recognizes (timers, promises, DOM events, XHR/fetch, etc.). Anything a third-party library does outside those — custom event systems, native code, iframe messaging in some configurations, or code that explicitly runs via `Zone.root` — isn't visible to it.

**Q: How would you solve this in a zoneless Angular app?**
Answer: Use signals for the relevant state. Writing to a signal notifies Angular's reactivity/scheduler directly, independent of which JS execution context the write happened in, so no `NgZone.run()` equivalent is needed.

**Q: Would `setTimeout(() => this.paymentStatus = 'success')` fix it?**
Answer: Only sometimes, and only by accident — `setTimeout` is Zone-patched, so if the SDK's callback context still allows Zone.js's monkeypatched globals to be used, wrapping in a fresh `setTimeout` executed from the zone-patched context might reschedule the callback back into the zone. It's fragile and not the correct fix — `ngZone.run()` is explicit and reliable.

**Q: What's `runOutsideAngular()` used for, and how does it relate to this bug?**
Answer: It's the opposite tool — used to deliberately execute code (e.g., high-frequency drag/scroll handlers) *without* triggering Angular's change detection, for performance. It's relevant here because it's a reminder that Angular's zone behavior is a deliberate, controllable mechanism in both directions, not just an automatic black box.

## 10. Interview-Ready Answer

"This is a classic zone-awareness bug. The payment SDK's success callback runs outside Angular's zone — it's third-party code that Zone.js never patched — so when the callback updates component state, the data is genuinely correct in memory, but Angular was never notified that anything changed, so it never runs change detection. The UI only updates later because some *other* zone-patched event, like a click anywhere on the page, incidentally triggers a global change detection pass that then picks up the already-stale-but-correct data. The fix is to explicitly re-enter Angular's zone around the state update, using `this.ngZone.run(() => { this.paymentStatus = 'success'; })` — injecting `NgZone` for that. That tells Angular a change happened inside its zone, so it automatically triggers change detection right after. For `OnPush` components I'd pair that with `ChangeDetectorRef.markForCheck()`, or skip zones entirely by using a signal for the state, since signal writes notify Angular's reactivity system directly regardless of execution context."

## 11. Quick Revision

**Quick Revision**
- What: UI doesn't update after a third-party SDK callback fires; needs an unrelated click to force a refresh.
- Why: Third-party code runs outside Zone.js's patched APIs, so Angular never learns a change happened.
- Problem solved: Reconnects the state change to Angular's change detection trigger mechanism.
- How: Wrap the update in `NgZone.run(() => {...})`, or call `ChangeDetectorRef.markForCheck()/detectChanges()`, or use signals (zoneless-safe by design).
- Use when: Any third-party SDK integration — payment gateways, maps, chat widgets, WebSocket libs, legacy jQuery plugins.
- Important: `NgZone.run()` ≠ `NgZone.runOutsideAngular()` — opposite purposes; don't mix them up in an answer.
- Remember: For `OnPush` components, being inside the zone isn't always enough — pair with `markForCheck()`.

## Memory Trick

Think of it as: Angular is a teacher who only reacts when a specific bell rings (Zone-patched events). The payment SDK changed something on the whiteboard (component state) but didn't ring the bell — so the teacher (Angular) doesn't turn around and update the class (DOM) until *some* bell (any click) eventually rings. `ngZone.run()` is you manually ringing the bell yourself right after making the change.
